<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Fleet keeper — one tick a minute with no Claude session as its parent.

  WHY A LAUNCHD AGENT, and not a detached loop or an in-session `Monitor`:

    * An in-session `Monitor` dies with the session it lives in. The whole point of the
      keeper is to survive the thing it watches, so a `Monitor` is structurally unable.
    * A detached loop is the exact failure mode being fixed: one long-lived process that
      can die silently and take the supervision with it.
    * `StartInterval` gives a FRESH PROCESS per tick, so there is no loop to die. This is
      the same shape `com.bborbe.fleet-digest-notify` already uses.

  The keeper's own liveness is a separate instrument — `~/.claude/state/fleet-keeper/heartbeat`,
  rewritten at the top of every tick. A dead keeper leaves that file ageing while
  `fleet-keepalive.py` may still read `ok`, which is the distinction the task's SC6 requires.

  Install:
    cp launchd/com.bborbe.fleet-keeper.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.bborbe.fleet-keeper.plist

  Reload after an upgrade:
    launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/com.bborbe.fleet-keeper

  The script path is the marketplace clone, not a version-pinned cache dir, for the same
  reason `fleet-digest-notify-tick.sh` uses it: a cache path would pin the agent to one
  release and silently run stale code after every upgrade.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.bborbe.fleet-keeper</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/bin/python3</string>
        <string>/Users/bborbe/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/claude-supervisor/scripts/fleet-keeper.py</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      ⚠️ The PATH is not decoration: launchd gives a job no login shell, so it never reads
      ~/.zshrc, and anything not on this list is simply not found.

      System directories ONLY, on purpose. The report-only keeper calls nothing but its sibling
      Python scripts (through `sys.executable`) and `ps` (in /bin). The restart-era keeper needed
      `vault-cli` and `wezterm` to resume a session; it has no resume path now, so neither is
      needed. User-writable directories (~/.local/bin, ~/Documents/workspaces/go/bin) would sit
      ahead of /usr/bin and let anything dropped there shadow a system tool.

      Measured 2026-11-01, the cost of getting this list wrong: with `vault-cli` missing, the
      restart-era keeper refused every tick for 715 ticks; with `wezterm` missing, it killed the
      Fleet Manager and could not resume it. Both failures read as a refusal, not as a broken
      environment. If a callee ever needs a new tool, add it here and say which callee needs it.
    -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>

    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>60</integer>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/bborbe/Library/Logs/fleet-keeper.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/bborbe/Library/Logs/fleet-keeper.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
